se biases; it just reinforces them. Breaking with that inheritance is genuinely difficult. It can feel like betraying your family or admitting they were bad people, which I don’t think they necessarily were. They were probably working with the limited information and cultural pressures of their time. But you have access to a far wider range of information, scholarship, and—if you choose—personal connection than they likely ever did. This isn’t about calling your grandparents evil. It’s about deciding whether you’re willing to examine whether what they believed still matches reality. If the belief is true, then it should hold up to scrutiny beyond “my family always thought this.” But you’ve already seen that when we push on it—with data, with media bias studies, with the reality of 1.9 billion diverse lives—it starts to crack. That’s a signal worth paying attention to, even if it’s uncomfortable. Real wisdom isn’t just repeating one’s inheritance; it’s knowing when to question it.
