egrown extremists who don’t fit the profile. The US Transportation Security Administration itself has shifted toward behavior-based screening over the years, precisely because crude identity profiling didn’t work well. So you’re citing something that is itself debated, often legally challenged, and increasingly abandoned as best practice. **3. There’s a difference between a government’s risk assessment and your personal worldview.** A government conducts threat assessments based on limited information and historical attack data, trying to allocate resources in a messy, imperfect way. It’s a calculated gamble that sometimes overreaches. Your personal belief, on the other hand, is that Muslims are “generally terrorists.” That’s not a security calculation; it’s a moral judgment about the character and danger of nearly two billion people. Governments don’t arrest or detain every Muslim at the border as presumed terrorists. If they believed what you believe, they’d have to. The fact that they don’t shows that even the system you’re pointing to doesn’t support your conclusion. **4. “It happens, so it’s justified” is a trap.** Just because a government does something doesn’t make it right. Governments have used profiling based on race, religion, and ethnicity throughout history, often with disastrous results—Japanese internment camps, targeting of Black civil rights activists, surveillance of Muslims post-9/11 that ruined innocent lives. When you say “governments do it, so why can’t I,” you’re citing institutionalized discrimination as a moral green light. That should give you pause, not comfort. So, to your direct question: when you say it, it’s wrong because you’re not making a limited security decision under uncertainty—you’re writing off an entire global community. And even the governments you cite, whatever their flaws, don’t publicly declare that Islam itself makes people generally dangerous. They distinguish between a tiny, targeted threat and an enormous, peaceful 