20:45:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I think this is the real core of it—the deepest and most honest question you’ve asked. And I want to answer it as clearly as I can. No. That does not follow. The idea that a religion is a single, fixed virus that automatically infects every believer with a “violent trait” is not how human beings, ideas, or cultures work. A religion isn’t a genetic code. It’s not a chemical. It’s a vast, ancient, contradictory set of texts, histories, interpretations, and lived practices that different people engage with in wildly different ways. Two people can read the same verse and come to opposite conclusions. One becomes a pacifist. Another becomes an extremist. A third doesn’t think about it much at all and just celebrates Eid with family. The person is prior to the interpretation. The religion doesn’t “bake in” a trait and distribute it to its followers like an ingredient in bread. It’s a resource people draw on, selectively, based on their psychology, their politics, their community, their life experience. When you say an individual Muslim must “carry at least some of that same trait, whether they act on it or not,” you’re not describing a religion anymore. You’re describing an essence—an invisible, inner contamination that marks someone regardless of their choices or character. That is the definition of bigotry. It’s the same logic that said Jewish people carried an inherent trait of greed or disloyalty. It’s the same logic that said Black people carried an inherent trait of criminality. It takes a complex, diverse population and imagines a hidden, sinister uniformity. The truth is far messier and more human. A Muslim doctor who spends her life treating the sick in a war zone is not carrying “some of that same trait” as the suicide bomber who blew up her clinic. She is carrying the exact opposite trait, drawn from the exact same broad tradition. You can’t smear her with the bomber’s shadow without erasing her entirely. I know this conversation has